@startuml data-model
' Rendered with PlantUML's built-in Smetana layout (pure Java) so the SVG export
' needs no external Graphviz/dot install — reproducible in CI and on any machine.
!pragma layout smetana
' arc42 §5 — Persistence data model (data view) of the SteuerEule Postgres schema.
' Source of record for the diagram; export to data-model.svg (see README).
' Highlighted (green) = added since this view's previous revision (REQ-001): the
' better-auth identity tables + RateLimit (Slice 2, ADR-0009/0012, REQ-005/006/009/010)
' and LegalHold (REQ-011 Löschschutz seam, ADR-0013 §5). Mirror of
' apps/api/prisma/schema.prisma — keep the two in step (stale arc42 = failing test).
hide circle
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam classBorderColor #6b7280

title SteuerEule — Persistence Data Model · arc42 §5 (data view)\ngreen = added since the REQ-001 revision (Slice 2 auth · REQ-011 Löschschutz)

note as Nscope
  **userId is a logical scoping key on the domain tables, not a DB foreign key**
  (ADR-0007). Profile / TaxDataAccessLog / TaxYear / LegalHold carry no FK to
  User: the same column holds either a **verified better-auth user id** or an
  **HMAC-signed guest id**, and only the UserContextGuard decides which. Every
  row is read scoped to a single userId; there are no cross-user joins.
  The better-auth tables below *do* use real FKs among themselves.
end note

class Profile {
  id : uuid <<PK>>
  userId : string <<unique>>
  firstName : string
  lastName : string
  steuerId : string <<encrypted>>
  steuernummer : string? <<encrypted>>
  createdAt : DateTime
  updatedAt : DateTime
}
note bottom of Profile
  steuerId / steuernummer are field-encrypted at rest
  (ADR-0008 — AES-256-GCM, per-write nonce, mode=strict).
  Plain facts only otherwise — clean side of the
  determinism boundary; @steuereule/core never recomputes these.
  **On account deletion the row is hard-deleted** (ADR-0013 §2):
  the ciphertext is genuinely gone, not soft-flagged. The
  field-encryption key is app-wide, so per-user crypto-shredding
  is not available — hard-delete is what makes erasure real.
end note

class TaxDataAccessLog <<append-only>> {
  id : uuid <<PK>>
  userId : string <<indexed>>
  action : AuditAction (READ | WRITE)
  resource : string
  createdAt : DateTime
}
note bottom of TaxDataAccessLog
  DSGVO Art. 30 accountability trail (ADR-0008 / REQ-004).
  Who/what/when only — no value column, never the plaintext
  or ciphertext. Audited surface is identifier-class access
  (Profile / export), not read-of-own-estimate (Cockpit).
  **On account deletion the rows are anonymised, not deleted**
  (ADR-0013 §1): userId is re-keyed to an irreversible
  `deleted:<uuid>` tombstone, so the processing record survives
  while the person-link does not (Recital 26). That UPDATE is
  the **second** sanctioned exception to app-layer append-only,
  after ADR-0012 §4's guest→account re-key.
end note

class TaxYear {
  id : uuid <<PK>>
  userId : string
  steuerjahr : int
  .. raw inputs only ..
  baseEstimate : int
  openItems : int
  openConflicts : int
  .. ..
  createdAt : DateTime
  updatedAt : DateTime
  __ unique (userId, steuerjahr) __
}
note bottom of TaxYear
  Stores **raw inputs only**. The Cockpit refund-estimate
  **range is never persisted** — recomputed on every read via
  @steuereule/core cockpitRange() (determinism boundary,
  ADR-014/048), so there is no second place to drift.
  No @encrypted field: ruled out of ADR-0008 scope (Musti
  review, R1a) — no tax identifier, only a rounded estimate
  and two counts. Revisit if assessed amounts / income land here.
end note

class LegalHold #palegreen {
  id : uuid <<PK>>
  userId : string <<indexed>>
  resource : string
  holdUntil : DateTime
  createdAt : DateTime
}
note bottom of LegalHold
  Minimal **Löschschutz** seam (ADR-0013 §5, product ADR-011).
  While holdUntil is in the future, the deletion transaction
  **exempts** that (userId, resource) from erasure/anonymisation
  and retains it under legal obligation. Today only "profile" /
  "auditLog" are meaningful and the exempt set is vacuous in real
  use — the mechanism is real and acceptance-tested on both
  branches, so the UI's retention promise is not a hypothetical.
end note

package "better-auth identity (ADR-0009 · schema owned by the library)" as ba {
  class User #palegreen {
    id : uuid <<PK>>
    name : string
    email : string <<unique>>
    emailVerified : boolean
    image : string?
    createdAt : DateTime
    updatedAt : DateTime
  }

  class Session #palegreen {
    id : uuid <<PK>>
    userId : string <<FK → User>>
    token : string <<unique>>
    expiresAt : DateTime
    ipAddress : string?
    userAgent : string?
    createdAt : DateTime
    updatedAt : DateTime
  }

  class Account #palegreen {
    id : uuid <<PK>>
    userId : string <<FK → User>>
    providerId : string
    accountId : string
    password : string? <<scrypt hash>>
    accessToken / refreshToken / idToken : string?
    scope : string?
    createdAt : DateTime
    updatedAt : DateTime
  }

  class Verification #palegreen {
    id : uuid <<PK>>
    identifier : string
    value : string
    expiresAt : DateTime
  }
}

User "1" -- "0..*" Session : onDelete: Cascade
User "1" -- "0..*" Account : onDelete: Cascade

note bottom of User
  Deleting this row is what makes an account **gone**: Session and
  Account cascade, Verification rows are removed by email, and the
  whole teardown runs inside the single deletion $transaction
  (ADR-0013 §3) together with Profile's hard-delete and the audit
  anonymisation — all-or-nothing.
end note

note bottom of Account
  Holds the **password hash** (scrypt, better-auth's own) and any
  social-provider tokens. **Never exported**: the DSGVO export
  assembles account *identity* only (email, name, emailVerified,
  providers) — no hash, no session/verification token, ever
  (ADR-0013 §4).
end note

class RateLimit #palegreen {
  id : uuid <<PK>>
  key : string?
  count : int?
  lastRequest : bigint?
}
note bottom of RateLimit
  DB-backed, deliberately **not** in-memory (REQ-010 / ADR-0012 §5):
  an in-memory counter resets per pod and is trivially bypassed once
  the API scales horizontally. Reused — not re-invented — by the
  deletion path's password re-verification limit (ADR-0013 §6 note).
end note

@enduml
